    h1: "How Is Vertical Mixed-Use Development Engineering Structured?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a vertical mixed-use building is really two or three buildings stacked in one envelope, each with its own code identity, structural behavior, and building systems. Retail at grade, offices or hospitality in the middle, and housing on top each bring different occupancy classifications, different hours of operation, and different MEP demands — and the engineering structures the building so those differences never collide. Direct answer: the engineer stacks the uses with separated structural systems (often a concrete podium carrying lighter framing above), divides them with rated horizontal and vertical fire separations, runs separate or independently metered MEP systems per occupancy, and plans vertical circulation so residents, office tenants, and retail customers never share the same paths.\n\nOccupancy classification is the first design decision. Under the IBC, each use is classified (mercantile, business, residential, assembly), and the design team chooses between separated occupancies — where each use is bounded by fire-rated construction and the most restrictive code provisions apply only within their own footprint — and non-separated occupancies, where the entire building inherits the most restrictive requirements of any use. Separated occupancies are the workhorse of vertical mixed-use because they let a five-story residential section sit over a two-story retail podium without the housing inheriting retail's sprinkler and egress rules. The engineer maps every separation on the plans: horizontal assemblies between stacked uses and vertical separations where uses sit side by side at grade.\n\nStructure follows the stacking logic. The classic arrangement is a concrete podium — one or two levels of retail and parking framed in concrete or structural steel — carrying a transfer slab or transfer girders, with four to five stories of lighter wood or steel framing above for the residential portion. The transfer level is the most engineered floor in the building: it collects the distributed column loads from the housing grid above and redirects them to the wider-spaced columns the retail level needs for open storefronts and parking below. The engineer sizes transfer members for the cumulative load, details the post-tensioning or reinforcement, and checks the transfer level's stiffness so differential movement between the two structural systems does not crack finishes or jam doors above.\n\nMEP systems are designed for independence between uses. Retail tenants turn over, reconfigure, and run high-load equipment; residents expect quiet, stable comfort and low utility bills they can meter individually. The engineer typically provides separate HVAC distribution per occupancy — dedicated rooftop or split systems for retail bays, and separate residential systems sized for the quieter, steadier housing loads — so a restaurant's exhaust upgrade never starves an apartment of ventilation air. Plumbing risers, electrical services, and fire protection are arranged so each occupancy can be shut down, serviced, or re-tenanted without touching the others. In California, the residential and nonresidential portions must each satisfy the 2025 California Energy Code / 2025 Standards, effective January 1, 2026. That often means different envelope and lighting compliance paths in the same building.",
