    h1: "How Is Mixed-Use Occupancy Separation Engineering Designed?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that occupancy separation is the code strategy that makes mixed-use legal: a deliberate choice, drawn on every plan, about how the building's different uses are divided from each other. The IBC offers two paths — separated occupancies, where rated construction divides the uses, and non-separated occupancies, where it does not — and the choice drives the fire ratings, sprinkler rules, egress design, and allowable area of the entire project. Direct answer: the engineer classifies each use, selects the separation approach that fits the project's economics and geometry, draws every required horizontal and vertical separation with its fire rating, and details every penetration, shaft, and opening so the separations hold in construction as drawn.\n\nSeparated occupancies are the standard for true mixed-use. Each occupancy is bounded by fire barriers (walls) and horizontal assemblies (floor/ceiling) with ratings from the IBC's separation table — typically 1 or 2 hours depending on the pairing, such as residential over mercantile. The payoff is that the most restrictive provisions of any single occupancy apply only within that occupancy's footprint: the housing does not inherit the retail's requirements and vice versa. The engineer tables every occupancy pairing in the building, pulls the required rating for each, and shows the separation lines on the life-safety plans so the code reviewer can trace each boundary.\n\nNon-separated occupancies have their place in lighter mixes. Where the uses are compatible — say, business offices over a small retail lobby, or a mercantile ground floor under business offices — the engineer may accept non-separated status, which eliminates the rated separations but subjects the whole building to the most restrictive requirements of any occupancy present. The math is a tradeoff: the cost and complexity of rated separations and their penetrations versus the cost of upgrading the entire building's sprinklers, alarms, and egress to the strictest use. The engineer runs both options early, because switching approaches mid-design redraws the life-safety plans.\n\nIncidental and accessory occupancies add a third layer. Small support uses — a residential leasing office inside an apartment building, a storage room in a retail block — can often be classified as accessory to the main occupancy, which exempts them from separation requirements up to area limits. The engineer identifies every such space and proves the accessory classification with area calculations, since misclassifying an incidental use is a common plan-check correction. Shaft enclosures, exit passageways, and elevator hoistways get their own ratings independent of the occupancy strategy, and the engineer coordinates those with the separation lines so a shaft does not accidentally breach a separation it was meant to protect.",
