        heading: "Transfer Slab and Girder Design",
        body: "The engineer starts with the two grids and the load map: every column and bearing wall from the housing above, with its service and factored loads, plotted against the podium columns and walls below that can receive them. The transfer element is then designed for the worst load path — usually a residential column landing mid-span of a transfer girder or midway between podium columns on a slab. Punching shear around each upper column is checked with the slab's full depth, and where the shear demand exceeds the concrete capacity, the engineer adds shear studs or drop panels rather than thickening the whole slab.\n\nDeflection governs as much as strength. The housing above tolerates very little differential movement: partitions crack, tile fails, and doors bind with fractions of an inch. The engineer computes short-term and long-term deflection including creep and shrinkage, and sets camber where needed so the finished slab lands flat. Post-tensioning is profiled to balance a chosen percentage of the dead load, and the stressing sequence is specified so the slab is never tensioned in a way that lifts it off its supports or overloads the podium columns below during construction.",
