        body: "The engineer treats the retail-to-housing boundary as an acoustic assembly first and a fire assembly second, designing both in the same construction. Airborne sound from the retail — music, conversation, kitchen clatter — is blocked by mass and airtightness: a concrete deck, sealed penetrations, and no rigid connections between the retail ceiling and the residential floor. Impact sound — footfall from a fitness class, dollies, dropped stock — needs decoupling: a floating floor, resilient channels, or an isolated ceiling system that breaks the vibration path. The engineer specifies the assembly to meet both the code's sound-transmission requirements and the developer's market standard, since luxury housing over retail demands better than code minimum.\n\nEquipment vibration gets its own isolation schedule. Every rotating machine in the retail — rooftop units, exhaust fans, refrigeration compressors, walk-in cooler condensers — is mounted on isolators selected for its weight and operating frequency, with flexible connectors on all ductwork and piping leaving the equipment. The engineer pays special attention to low-frequency sources like gyms and loading docks, whose energy travels efficiently through concrete structure; a fitness tenant above a bedroom stack may need a floating slab in the gym itself. The isolation details are shown on the drawings because field improvisation with the wrong isolator is a common source of post-occupancy noise complaints.",
