        body: "The engineer draws three circulation systems and keeps them from touching. The hotel system moves guests from arrival through lobby to guestroom floors, with public access to restaurants, meeting rooms, and amenities. The residential system moves owners from a secured entry to their elevator bank to their front doors, with fob or biometric control at every threshold. The service system moves staff, carts, linen, and deliveries through back-of-house corridors and freight elevators sized for banquet equipment and housekeeping operations. Where the systems must cross — a shared loading dock, a shared parking garage — the engineer designs the crossing with access control, separate elevator lobbies, and scheduling so hotel deliveries and resident move-ins never compete for the same dock.\n\nSecurity design follows the circulation. The residential elevator bank opens only to the residential lobby; hotel guests cannot call a residential car. Amenity spaces are zoned: the hotel pool and fitness center may be shared by agreement, but the residential-only amenities are access-controlled separately, and the engineer coordinates the access-control, CCTV, and intercom systems so each operator — hotel management and the residential association — controls its own domain. Fire alarm and egress are common to the building but zoned so a hotel kitchen alarm does not evacuate the residences unnecessarily, and vice versa.",
