        heading: "Power, Fire Protection, and In-Unit Separation",
        body: "The electrical design gives each unit two personalities. The living side gets a standard dwelling-unit panel with arc-fault and ground-fault protection per the residential code. The studio side gets a dedicated subpanel with 240-volt capacity, higher amperage, and circuits labeled for equipment — kiln, welder, compressor, dust collector — so the artist is not running a kiln off a bedroom receptacle circuit. The engineer totals the studio loads across the building honestly: fifty kilns firing on the same evening is a real coincident load, and the service is sized for a studied diversity rather than wishful thinking.\n\nFire protection reflects the hybrid hazard. The building is sprinklered throughout, and the studio side may need higher-hazard coverage depending on the processes allowed — the engineer confirms the hazard classification with the fire reviewer rather than assuming light-hazard everywhere. Flammable storage gets listed cabinets and quantity limits; the in-unit separation between studio and living areas carries a fire rating with a self-closing door; and the engineer coordinates the allowable-process list with the developer's lease, since the building's systems are designed for specific processes and a new process — say, someone taking up welding in a painting studio — needs engineering review before it starts.",
