    title: "How Is Parking Integrated Into Mixed-Use Development Design?",
    description: "Mixed-use parking integration engineering shares stalls across uses by demand timing, stacking garages under podiums with separated resident and public access.",
    h1: "How Is Parking Integrated Into Mixed-Use Development Design?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that parking is the shared resource that makes mixed-use pencil out: offices need stalls on weekday mornings, retail needs them on weekend afternoons, and housing needs them overnight, so one integrated garage serves all three with fewer stalls than the sum of their separate requirements. The engineering proves that sharing works, structures the garage to serve stacked uses above, and keeps the user groups from colliding. Direct answer: the engineer runs a shared-parking demand analysis to size the garage, designs the structure for the heaviest use above it, separates resident, office, and public parking zones with access control, and integrates ventilation, drainage, and EV charging for the combined demand.\n\nThe shared-parking analysis is the sizing instrument. The engineer models each use's parking demand by hour and day — office at 90 percent occupancy Tuesday at 10 a.m., retail peaking Saturday afternoon, residential peaking at 2 a.m. — using published ratios adjusted for the project's transit access and local mode share. The garage is sized for the combined peak hour, which is typically 20 to 30 percent smaller than the sum of the individual peaks. Most jurisdictions accept this methodology and grant the reduction, but the engineer documents the assumptions and the enforceable shared-parking agreement, since the reduction has to survive a future sale or use change.\n\nStructurally, the garage is the foundation of the vertical mix. In a podium project the parking sits in the concrete podium levels, designed for the transfer loads of the housing above, with floor-to-floor heights that work for both cars and the structure — typically 10 to 12 feet clear. The engineer designs the garage slab for the concentrated loads of the transfer columns, details the waterproofing at the podium top where the garage meets the occupied levels above, and slopes every deck to drains because a garage that ponds water destroys its own structure. Ramps are laid out for the design vehicle's turning radius with sight lines at every turn, and the engineer checks headroom at every beam, sign, and sprinkler line along the drive path.\n\nVentilation, drainage, and EV charging are designed for the combined occupancy. Enclosed garages need mechanical ventilation sized to the vehicle count and the code's air-change or contaminant-sensor requirements, with the exhaust routed so it never enters residential intakes or operable windows above. EV charging is now a major electrical load: the engineer sizes the service and the distribution for the jurisdiction's required percentage of EV-ready stalls plus a growth allowance, with load management so fifty cars charging overnight do not demand fifty cars' worth of simultaneous service capacity. Drainage includes oil-water separation where the jurisdiction requires it, and the engineer keeps garage runoff out of the residential storm system where the codes treat them differently.",
    directAnswer: "Mixed-use parking is engineered as one shared garage sized by combined-peak demand analysis, structured to carry the building above, zoned by user group with access control, and equipped with ventilation, drainage, and managed EV charging for all uses at once.",
