        body: "Every vertical shaft that crosses an occupancy boundary is designed as a rated enclosure with protected openings at each floor. Elevator hoistways, mechanical shafts, plumbing chases, and trash chutes each get the rating the code assigns — independent of the occupancy separation rating, with the more restrictive governing where they overlap. The engineer details the shaft walls, the floor-by-floor opening protection, and the top and bottom terminations, since an open shaft top turns the enclosure into a flue. Trash chutes receive extra scrutiny: the intake doors are rated and self-closing, the shaft terminates in a sprinklered collection room, and the engineer confirms the chute manufacturer's listing matches the shaft rating.\n\nStair pressurization keeps the evacuation route tenable. In mid- and high-rise mixed-use, the stairs serving the residential floors are typically pressurized so that when doors open during evacuation, air flows out of the stair rather than smoke flowing in. The engineer designs the pressurization fans, the duct distribution, and the relief paths, and coordinates with the fire alarm sequence so the system activates on the right signal. Elevator recall, smoke detection zoning per occupancy, and the fire-fighter's smoke-control panel are all part of the same coordinated design — the separations contain the fire, and the smoke systems keep the exits usable while it is contained.",
