    title: "How Is Utility Metering Designed for Mixed-Use Buildings?",
    description: "Mixed-use metering engineering gives each occupancy separate electric, gas, and water meters, plus submeters on shared systems for fair measured cost splits.",
    h1: "How Is Utility Metering Designed for Mixed-Use Buildings?",
    answer: "The answer: Utility metering for mixed-use is engineered as a complete measurement architecture: utility meters per occupancy at each service entrance, submeters on every shared system, and meter locations and types matched to the building's legal cost-allocation structure.\n\nThe engineering answer is that metering is the financial plumbing of mixed-use: the meters and submeters that prove how much electricity, gas, and water each occupancy actually used, so the restaurant's gas bill never lands on the residents and the office's after-hours cooling never lands on the retail. Without designed-in metering, cost allocation becomes estimation — and estimation becomes dispute. Direct answer: the engineer provides utility-company meters at each occupancy's service entrance, submeters on the major shared systems, and a metering architecture that matches the building's legal structure — separate accounts where the utilities allow it, master-metered with submetering where they do not.\n\nElectric metering starts at the service. Where the utility allows, each occupancy gets its own service and its own utility meter — the cleanest arrangement, with each use billed directly. Where a single service feeds the building, the engineer provides a main utility meter plus submeters on each occupancy's switchboard or feeder, and the owner bills the tenants from the submeter readings. The design includes the meter sockets, current-transformer cabinets, and clearances the utility's service requirements demand, coordinated early because the utility's meter layout often dictates the electrical room's size and arrangement.\n\nGas and water follow the same logic with their own constraints. Gas utilities typically require separate meters per occupancy for commercial loads, and the engineer routes separate gas services or metered branches to the retail — especially restaurants — and to the residential boilers or water heaters. Water metering separates domestic use per occupancy plus dedicated irrigation and fire-service metering where the jurisdiction requires it. The engineer locates meters for utility access — many utilities require exterior or readily accessible meter locations — and protects them from the building's operations.\n\nShared-system submetering is where the engineering gets detailed. The central chiller plant, the site lighting, the parking garage ventilation, the shared elevators — all draw power that must be split between occupancies per the condo documents or leases. The engineer places submeters or BTU meters on each shared system's feeders and branches, with the meter readings tied into the building automation system where the owner wants automated allocation. The metering one-line shows every meter, its type, and what it measures, so the accountant's allocation spreadsheet has a physical counterpart in the building.",
    directAnswer: "Utility metering for mixed-use is engineered as a complete measurement architecture: utility meters per occupancy at each service entrance, submeters on every shared system, and meter locations and types matched to the building's legal cost-allocation structure.",
