    description: "Restaurant exhaust engineering in mixed-use routes grease air through rated shafts to the roof, with makeup air and filtration protecting residences above.",
    h1: "How Is Restaurant Exhaust Engineered in Mixed-Use Buildings?",
    answer: "The answer: Restaurant exhaust in mixed-use is engineered as a dedicated grease system: listed Type I hood capture, welded grease duct in a fire-rated shaft run directly to the roof, balanced tempered makeup air, and roof termination with filtration placed to protect every residential window and intake.\n\nThe engineering answer is that a restaurant inside a mixed-use building is an industrial process sitting under people's bedrooms: commercial cooking produces grease-laden vapors, heat, and odors at volumes no residential system was meant to handle, and the exhaust has to travel from the kitchen, through the occupied building, to the roof without endangering or annoying anyone along the way. Direct answer: the engineer captures the cooking effluent with a listed Type I hood, routes it through a dedicated fire-rated grease duct shaft straight to the roof, provides tempered makeup air to the kitchen, and terminates the exhaust well clear of residential windows, balconies, and air intakes.\n\nThe hood is the capture point and the design starts there. The engineer sizes the Type I hood to the cooking equipment — length, overhang, and exhaust rate per the code and the hood listing — and verifies the capture with the equipment layout, since an undersized or poorly positioned hood lets grease escape into the kitchen and the building. The exhaust rate for a commercial kitchen dwarfs the ventilation of the rest of the retail bay, and the engineer sizes the grease duct, the fan, and the makeup air as one balanced system: every cubic foot exhausted is replaced with tempered outdoor air so the kitchen does not go negative and pull air from the dining room or the residences above.\n\nThe grease duct shaft is the most regulated duct in the building. The mechanical code requires grease ducts to be built of welded steel, enclosed in a fire-rated shaft where they pass through the building, with cleanout access at every change of direction and no dampers or obstructions inside. The engineer routes the shaft as directly as possible from the kitchen to the roof — every offset is a grease-collection point and a cleaning burden — and keeps it independent of every other duct system in the building. Where the shaft passes the residential floors, the rated enclosure and the sealed construction keep a duct fire contained in the shaft.\n\nTermination and filtration protect the residents. The exhaust fan discharges above the roof through an upblast fan listed for grease duty, terminating the code-required distance from property lines, operable windows, and — critically — from the building's own residential intakes, operable windows, and balconies. The engineer studies the prevailing wind and the roof geometry so the plume does not wash across the residential terraces. Where the jurisdiction or the developer demands extra odor control, the engineer adds filtration — electrostatic precipitators or carbon modules — while accounting for the added static pressure in the fan selection and the maintenance the filters require.",
    directAnswer: "Restaurant exhaust in mixed-use is engineered as a dedicated grease system: listed Type I hood capture, welded grease duct in a fire-rated shaft run directly to the roof, balanced tempered makeup air, and roof termination with filtration placed to protect every residential window and intake.",
