    slug: "mixed-use-accessibility-design",
    title: "How Is Accessibility Designed Across Mixed-Use Occupancies?",
    description: "Mixed-use accessibility engineering provides independent ADA routes, entrances, and amenities per occupancy so each use complies without depending on another.",
    h1: "How Is Accessibility Designed Across Mixed-Use Occupancies?",
    answer: "The answer: Accessibility across mixed-use occupancies is engineered as independent compliant systems per use: separate accessible entrances and routes, Fair Housing adaptable units plus code-required accessible units in the housing, per-tenant commercial compliance, and site routes that never cross another occupancy's secured area.\n\nThe engineering answer is that accessibility in mixed-use is not one accessible route but several: each occupancy must provide its own compliant entrances, paths, restrooms, and amenities, because the retail's accessible entrance cannot serve the residences and the office's accessible restrooms cannot serve the apartments. The design gives every use an independent, dignified accessible experience. Direct answer: the engineer designs separate accessible entrances and primary routes for each occupancy, provides the code-required accessible dwelling units and Type B units in the residential portion, ensures each commercial use has its own accessible restrooms and service counters, and keeps every accessible path independent of the other occupancies' secured areas.\n\nResidential accessibility follows the Fair Housing Act and the building code together. Ground-floor units in elevator buildings — and effectively all units where an elevator serves the floor — must meet the adaptable-unit requirements: accessible entrances, usable doors, accessible routes through the unit, reinforced bathroom walls for grab bars, and usable kitchens and bathrooms. A percentage of units goes further to full accessibility per the code. The engineer works with the architect on the unit plans early, because an accessible bathroom layout or a compliant kitchen clear-floor space cannot be retrofitted into a finished unit plan without redrawing it.\n\nCommercial accessibility is per occupancy and per tenant space. Each retail bay needs its own accessible entrance, accessible route through the sales area, and accessible service counter; the office floors need accessible routes, restrooms, and work-area accommodations per the tenant's obligations; restaurants need accessible seating dispersed through the dining area and accessible restrooms. The engineer verifies the base-building provisions — entrances, common restrooms, site routes — and documents the tenant's improvement obligations so each TI knows where the base building stops and the tenant's compliance starts.\n\nSite accessibility ties the occupancies together without mixing them. The accessible parking is distributed per use — retail accessible stalls near the retail entrance, residential near the residential entrance — with accessible routes from each to its own entrance that do not force a wheelchair user through another occupancy's secured area or across the service drive. The engineer grades the site so the accessible routes hold their slopes within the code's limits, details the curb ramps and detectable warnings, and keeps the accessible path of travel clear of the loading and trash operations that share the site.",
    directAnswer: "Accessibility across mixed-use occupancies is engineered as independent compliant systems per use: separate accessible entrances and routes, Fair Housing adaptable units plus code-required accessible units in the housing, per-tenant commercial compliance, and site routes that never cross another occupancy's secured area.",
