        body: "The pedestrian plan is drawn as a network with the same rigor as the drives. The engineer establishes the primary accessible routes — transit stop to each entrance, parking to each entrance, entrance to entrance — at compliant slopes with rest areas where the distances are long, and lights them to the levels the security plan requires. Crossings of public drives get marked crosswalks with pedestrian-scale lighting; crossings of the service loop are eliminated by routing, not by paint. The plaza and courtyard spaces are designed as pedestrian-priority zones with mountable curbs or bollards rather than open drive aisles, so a delivery driver cannot mistake them for a shortcut.\n\nFire access overlays everything. The engineer designates the fire lanes on the site plan with the required widths — typically 20 feet minimum, wider where the fire authority requires aerial apparatus access — verifies the inside turning radius for the authority's largest truck, and confirms the overhead clearance along the full lane. Every building face is checked against the hose-lay distance from the lane, hydrants are placed for coverage, and the fire-department connections are located on the lanes with clear staging space. The engineer submits the fire-access plan to the fire reviewer with the site plan, since fire access comments reshape circulation and are cheapest to address before the civil drawings advance.",
